        <a href="#references">References</a>
    </nav>

    <main>

        <!-- OVERVIEW -->
        <section id="overview">

            <h2>Overview</h2>

            <p>
                The 1973 Constitution of the Philippines established
                the Judiciary under Article X. Judicial power was
                vested in one Supreme Court and in such inferior courts
                as may be established by law.
            </p>

            <div class="cards">

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Article</h3>
                    <p>
                        Article X of the 1973 Constitution dealt with
                        the Judiciary.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Composition</h3>
                    <p>
                        The Supreme Court consisted of a Chief Justice
                        and fourteen Associate Justices.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Total Members</h3>
                    <p>
                        The Court had a total of fifteen members.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Court Divisions</h3>
                    <p>
                        The Supreme Court could sit en banc or in
                        two divisions.
                    </p>
                </div>

            </div>

        </section>

        <!-- STRUCTURE -->
        <section id="structure">

            <h2>Structure of the Supreme Court</h2>

            <p>
                Under Article X, Section 2 of the 1973 Constitution,
                the Supreme Court was composed of one Chief Justice
                and fourteen Associate Justices.
            </p>

            <h3>Organization</h3>

            <ul>
                <li>Chief Justice</li>
                <li>Fourteen Associate Justices</li>
                <li>Fifteen members in total</li>
                <li>Could sit en banc</li>
                <li>Could sit in two divisions</li>
            </ul>

        </section>

        <!-- QUALIFICATIONS -->
        <section id="qualifications">

            <h2>Qualifications of Supreme Court Members</h2>

            <p>
                The 1973 Constitution established qualifications
                for membership in the Supreme Court.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    Must be a natural-born citizen of the Philippines.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Must be at least 40 years of age.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Must have been a judge of a court of record or
                    engaged in the practice of law in the Philippines
                    for at least ten years.
                </li>
            </ul>

        </section>

        <!-- POWERS -->
        <section id="powers">

            <h2>Powers and Jurisdiction</h2>

            <p>
                Article X, Section 5 described important powers and
                jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.
            </p>

            <table>

                <tr>
                    <th>Area</th>
                    <th>Description</th>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Original Jurisdiction</td>
                    <td>
                        Cases involving ambassadors, other public
                        ministers and consuls, and petitions involving
                        extraordinary writs such as certiorari,
                        prohibition, mandamus, quo warranto and
                        habeas corpus.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Constitutional Questions</td>
                    <td>
                        Review of cases involving the constitutionality
                        or validity of laws, treaties, executive
                        agreements and certain government issuances.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Appellate Review</td>
                    <td>
                        Review of certain cases decided by inferior
                        courts according to the constitutional
                        jurisdiction of the Court.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Rule-Making</td>
                    <td>
                        Promulgation of rules concerning pleading,
                        practice and procedure, subject to
                        constitutional limitations.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>Administrative Supervision</td>
                    <td>
                        Administrative supervision over all courts
                        and their personnel.
                    </td>
                </tr>

            </table>

        </section>

        <!-- JUDICIAL ADMINISTRATION -->
        <section>

            <h2>Judicial Administration</h2>

            <p>
                The Supreme Court exercised administrative supervision
                over all courts and their personnel. It also had
                authority concerning the discipline of judges of
                inferior courts.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    Supervision of the lower courts.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Supervision of court personnel.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Authority to promulgate judicial rules.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Authority concerning the discipline of judges
                    under the constitutional framework.
                </li>
            </ul>

        </section>

        <!-- TIMELINE -->
        <section id="timeline">

            <h2>Historical Timeline</h2>

            <div class="timeline">

                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="year">1973</div>
                    <p>
                        The 1973 Constitution was ratified and
                        established its constitutional framework
                        for the Judiciary under Article X.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="year">1973–1986</div>
                    <p>
                        The Supreme Court operated under the
                        constitutional framework of the 1973
                        Constitution, as amended.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="year">1986</div>
                    <p>
                        A transitional government was established
                        following the change of government. The
                        Provisional Constitution adopted Article X
                        of the 1973 Constitution, as amended, subject
                        to its consistency with the Provisional
                        Constitution.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="year">1987</div>
                    <p>
                        The 1987 Constitution replaced the
                        transitional constitutional framework and
                        established the present constitutional
                        structure of the Judiciary.
                    </p>
                </div>

            </div>

        </section>

        <!-- SIGNIFICANCE -->
        <section>

            <h2>Historical Significance</h2>

            <p>
                The Supreme Court under the 1973 Constitution formed
                an important part of Philippine constitutional and
                judicial history. The Constitution defined the
                Court's composition, qualifications, jurisdiction,
                rule-making authority, and administrative supervision.
            </p>

            <p>
                Studying this period helps explain the development
                of the Philippine Judiciary and the transition from
                the 1973 constitutional framework to the 1986
                transitional government and eventually the
                1987 Constitution.
            </p>

        </section>

